# Vauxhall Big Six saloon, 1934-1940.
#
# The Big Six: the BX 2,393 cc and BY 3,180 cc sixes, with the BXL long
# wheelbase, and the GY/GL "Twenty Five" of 3,215 cc from 1937. The standard
# saloon on the 111 in chassis is modelled; the long-wheelbase BXL, the
# Hurlingham and Rye coupes, the Newmarket seven-seater and the Grosvenor
# limousine are not represented.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vauxhall_Big_Six
#
# Dimensions: wheelbase 111 in (2.819 m) or 130 in (3.302 m) for the BXL;
# length 170 in (4.318 m) or 184 in (4.674 m). The short chassis is modelled.
# Width and height were not established from a source.
#
# Shape reference: NO true level profile exists. Image 1 is about 30 degrees
# off the side and is the long-wheelbase BXL limousine, not the standard
# saloon, so the flank is estimated and the body mismatch is recorded.
#
# Colours: grade (d); no Vauxhall colour list was found. Black, Maroon, Cream
# and Dark Blue are observed colours. Weights 14, 10, 8, 8, total 40,
# provisional.
#
# Speed 120 km/h. Comfort 40/390, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 40. Classes 2,3.
# Dates: intro January 1934; retires staggered 1952-1955 as a road tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/vauxhall-big-six.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/vauxhall-big-six.dae. References:
# citycars/references/vauxhall-big-six/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the short-chassis BX/BY five-seat saloon is modelled,
# not the BXL limousine, the Holden sedan or the later GY/GL. The rear glazing
# and fittings are approximate because no direct standard-saloon rear
# photograph exists.


